MASTER BUILD GUIDE.md

Project: Gemini Playground – Universal Runtime Environment
Revision: v1.0 (Full handoff edition)
Author: Doc + AI Dev Pipeline
Date: 2025-10-19

🔭 Purpose

This guide is the complete handoff and build contract for the Gemini Playground application.
It merges every known rule, architectural detail, coding standard, and implementation plan into one file.
Anyone—human or AI—should be able to open this document, load the repo, and resume development safely without losing functionality or context.

1 | Project Identity

Gemini Playground is a multi-language, AI-enhanced execution and visualization environment.
It provides:

Real-time code editing and preview.

Dynamic runtime selection (Gemini, OpenAI, Node, Python etc.).

Unified logging, telemetry, and AI-assisted development.

Plugin-driven architecture that can self-extend at runtime.

2 | Core Development Rules & Ethos
2.1 Full-File Rule

Whenever new code is delivered:

Always output the full file contents.

Include absolute file path (e.g. /core/runtimeManager.js).

Indicate whether the file is 🆕 New, 🔁 Replacement, or 🗑️ Deleted.

Include the exact line count after the code block.

2.2 Preserve Functionality

Do not remove user-written functions unless absolutely necessary.
If a section must be rewired or replaced, it must either:

Call the original function internally, or

Re-implement the same interface so no caller breaks.

2.3 Change Transparency

Each modification must include:

Brief summary of what changed and why.

Risk level (low / medium / high).

Migration notes (if APIs or data paths changed).

2.4 Execution Policy

Code must remain portable across Windows / macOS / Linux.

No hard-coded paths outside the project root.

Use relative imports and ESM syntax consistently.

All network calls must pass through centralized modules (webFetch.js, aiService.js).

2.5 Testing Before Replacement

Every replacement file must compile and execute cleanly before overwriting production versions.
Temporary staging directory: /sandbox/replace_test/.

2.6 Coding Standards

Tabs: 2 spaces.

Naming: camelCase for functions, PascalCase for components.

Comments: use doc-block format (/** … */) for top-level functions and modules.

Each file starts with a header comment stating path + purpose.

2.7 Behavioral Rules for AI Assistants

Never truncate code.

Never remove functional wiring Doc built manually.

Always infer intent from existing code patterns before proposing restructure.

No "surface fixes"—address root logic.

If logic is uncertain, flag the line with // VERIFY_BEFORE_COMMIT.

