10 | Plugin Ecosystem Blueprint
10.1 Philosophy

Everything that isn’t essential core logic belongs in a plugin.
A plugin can extend UI, AI, Runtimes, Workflows, or Storage without changing internal files.
They load dynamically from /plugins/ and self-register through the pluginManager.

Goal: Make modification composable, not brittle.

10.2 Plugin File Structure
/plugins/
 ├── autoFormatter.js
 ├── runtimePython.js
 ├── aiPromptTools.js
 ├── uiThemeSwitcher.js
 └── ...


Each file exports:

export const meta = {
  id: "autoFormatter",
  name: "Auto Formatter",
  version: "1.0.0",
  description: "Formats code automatically on save",
  author: "Doc"
}

export function register(ctx) {
  // ctx gives access to core hooks
  ctx.on("file:save", (file) => formatFile(file));
  ctx.ui.addButton("Format", formatActiveFile);
}

export function unregister(ctx) {
  ctx.off("file:save", formatFile);
}

10.3 Plugin Manager

File: /core/pluginManager.js
Purpose: discover, validate, and manage plugin lifecycles.

Lifecycle:

discover() → validate() → register() → enable/disable()


Registers available plugins in /config/plugins.json:

{
  "autoFormatter": true,
  "aiPromptTools": false
}


Each loaded plugin gets its own sandbox context (ctx) with limited access to core APIs:

{
  ai: { generate, registerProvider },
  runtime: { run },
  ui: { addButton, addPanel },
  events: { on, off, emit }
}

10.4 Registration Flow

On startup, pluginManager.loadAll() scans /plugins/.

For each file:

import dynamically via import(pathToPlugin)

verify meta fields and register() exist

call register(ctx)

Plugin becomes active and logged in /logs/plugin.log

Example log:

[2025-10-19 12:34:01] Loaded plugin: Auto Formatter v1.0.0 (active)

10.5 Context Hooks
Hook	Description
on(event, fn)	subscribe to events (file:save, runtime:run, ai:response)
off(event, fn)	unsubscribe
emit(event, data)	broadcast custom events
ui.addButton(label, action)	inject toolbar button
ui.addPanel(id, component)	add new drawer pane
ai.generate(prompt, opts)	call AI service directly
runtime.run(lang, code)	execute code via runtime
config.get(key) / config.set(key, value)	read/write app config
10.6 Security Boundaries

Every plugin executes in its own VM context:

File I/O restricted to /workspace/ and /plugins/<id>/data/.

No require() outside safe modules.

No network access unless whitelisted in manifest:

"permissions": ["network"]


Logs written to /logs/plugins/<id>.log.

If plugin misbehaves → auto-disable and alert via alerts.js.

10.7 UI Integration

Plugins can extend the dashboard:

ctx.ui.addPanel("formatter", <FormatterPanel />);


The system mounts it dynamically inside PluginManager.tsx.

Available regions:

Region	Description
toolbar	icon buttons
leftDrawer	persistent panes
rightDrawer	collapsible panes
commandPalette	keyword extensions

Plugins register a manifest tag:

meta.ui = ["toolbar", "commandPalette"]

10.8 AI-Enhanced Plugins

Some plugins delegate tasks to AI:

autoCommenter → uses ai.generate("add docstrings to ...")

promptMemory → stores past interactions

refactorAdvisor → diffs code and suggests simplifications

These use the same ctx.ai.generate call, which respects rate-limits and API keys.

10.9 Plugin Events

Global event registry (/core/events.js):

Event	Payload	Trigger
file:save	{path}	File saved in editor
runtime:run	{lang, code}	User executes code
ai:response	{provider, text}	AI returns output
ui:ready	none	App rendered
plugin:error	{id, message}	Plugin failed runtime
10.10 Diagnostics

npm run test:plugins
Checks:

manifest fields present

register/unregister valid

permissions safe

runtime invocations mocked

Reports → /logs/plugin_test.log.

10.11 Versioning & Distribution

Each plugin has its own semver and optional auto-update URL.
Registry planned under /plugins/registry.json:

{
  "autoFormatter": {
    "version": "1.0.1",
    "updateUrl": "https://example.com/autoFormatter.json"
  }
}


Future: online marketplace integration for community plugins.

10.12 Plugin Lifecycle
Phase	Function	Description
Load	register()	initialize plugin context
Enable	ctx.enable()	start listening for events
Disable	unregister()	remove hooks
Update	ctx.reload()	hot reload without restart
Uninstall	ctx.remove()	delete plugin data
10.13 Error Handling

If plugin throws:

Catch in pluginManager

Log stack trace → /logs/plugin.log

Notify user via UI banner (Plugin <name> disabled)

Auto-disable entry in plugins.json

Plugins are isolated so a crash never takes down the editor.

10.14 Example Plugin: AI-Powered Diff Explainer

/plugins/aiDiffExplainer.js

export const meta = {
  id: "aiDiffExplainer",
  name: "AI Diff Explainer",
  version: "1.0.0",
  description: "Explains code changes using Gemini",
  permissions: ["ai"]
}

export function register(ctx) {
  ctx.on("diff:open", async (change) => {
    const prompt = `Explain this code diff in plain English:\n${change}`;
    const res = await ctx.ai.generate(prompt);
    ctx.ui.addPanel("diffExplain", <p>{res.text}</p>);
  });
}

10.15 Future Plugin Types

Data Plugins: connect external sources (e.g., Notion, GitHub)

Model Plugins: register custom AI providers

Runtime Plugins: introduce new languages

UI Plugins: visual themes and widgets

Workflow Plugins: automate multi-step tasks

10.16 Plugin Checklist (Before Merge)

 meta block complete

 register() / unregister() defined

 No direct imports from renderer core

 Permissions declared

 Logs to its own file

 Passes npm run test:plugins


11 | Server & Backend Integration Blueprint
11.1 Core Goals

The server side must:

Run independently of the UI (headless mode).

Expose clean endpoints for runtime execution, AI requests, and plugin sync.

Log everything for reproducibility and debugging.

Be replaceable — another process, container, or local node instance should all behave the same.

11.2 Directory Layout
server/
 ├── index.js           # Main entrypoint
 ├── api/
 │    ├── ai.js         # Gemini + OpenAI endpoints
 │    ├── runtime.js    # Execute code snippets
 │    ├── plugins.js    # Plugin registry sync
 │    ├── files.js      # File upload, diff, and asset handling
 │    └── metrics.js    # Usage telemetry
 ├── ws/
 │    ├── bridge.js     # WebSocket message handling
 │    └── events.js     # Internal event bus
 ├── utils/
 │    ├── rateLimit.js
 │    ├── logger.js
 │    ├── security.js
 │    └── env.js
 └── middleware/
      ├── auth.js
      ├── parseBody.js
      ├── errorHandler.js
      └── cors.js

11.3 Server Initialization

/server/index.js

import express from "express";
import http from "http";
import { Server } from "socket.io";
import path from "path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "url";
import { applyMiddlewares } from "./middleware/stack.js";
import { registerRoutes } from "./api/index.js";
import { attachWebSocket } from "./ws/bridge.js";

const __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
const __dirname = path.dirname(__filename);

const app = express();
const server = http.createServer(app);
const io = new Server(server, { cors: { origin: "*" } });

applyMiddlewares(app);
registerRoutes(app);
attachWebSocket(io);

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 5050;
server.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`[Server] Running on port ${PORT}`));

11.4 Middleware Stack

middleware/stack.js

import express from "express";
import cors from "cors";
import { parseBody } from "./parseBody.js";
import { errorHandler } from "./errorHandler.js";
import { rateLimit } from "../utils/rateLimit.js";

export function applyMiddlewares(app) {
  app.use(cors());
  app.use(express.json({ limit: "5mb" }));
  app.use(rateLimit());
  app.use(parseBody);
  app.use(errorHandler);
}


Handles parsing, CORS, rate limits, and error recovery globally.

All route handlers return structured JSON {ok, data, error}.

11.5 API Routes
AI

/api/ai.js

import express from "express";
import { generate } from "../../core/aiService.js";

const router = express.Router();

router.post("/generate", async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { prompt, options } = req.body;
    const result = await generate(prompt, options);
    res.json({ ok: true, data: result });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).json({ ok: false, error: err.message });
  }
});

export default router;

Runtime

/api/runtime.js

import express from "express";
import { run } from "../../core/runtimeManager.js";

const router = express.Router();

router.post("/execute", async (req, res) => {
  const { lang, code } = req.body;
  try {
    const result = await run(lang, code);
    res.json({ ok: true, data: result });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(400).json({ ok: false, error: err.message });
  }
});

export default router;

Plugins

/api/plugins.js

Returns active plugins and their statuses.

Allows upload or reload of new .js plugin files.

import express from "express";
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import { reloadPlugins } from "../../core/pluginManager.js";

const router = express.Router();
const pluginDir = path.resolve("plugins");

router.get("/", (req, res) => {
  const list = fs.readdirSync(pluginDir).filter(f => f.endsWith(".js"));
  res.json({ ok: true, data: list });
});

router.post("/reload", async (req, res) => {
  await reloadPlugins();
  res.json({ ok: true, message: "Plugins reloaded" });
});

export default router;

Metrics

/api/metrics.js

import express from "express";
import fs from "fs";
const router = express.Router();

router.get("/", (req, res) => {
  const logs = fs.readFileSync("./logs/runtime_logs/summary.json", "utf8");
  res.json({ ok: true, data: JSON.parse(logs) });
});

export default router;

11.6 WebSocket Bridge

Purpose: real-time updates between core, AI, and UI.
File: /server/ws/bridge.js

import { EventEmitter } from "events";

export const eventBus = new EventEmitter();

export function attachWebSocket(io) {
  io.on("connection", socket => {
    console.log(`[WS] Connected: ${socket.id}`);
    socket.on("runtime:run", data => eventBus.emit("runtime:run", data));
    socket.on("ai:prompt", data => eventBus.emit("ai:prompt", data));

    eventBus.on("runtime:output", data => socket.emit("runtime:output", data));
    eventBus.on("ai:response", data => socket.emit("ai:response", data));
  });
}

11.7 Internal Metrics System

Logs runtime/AI stats to /logs/metrics/

Generates daily aggregates for Reports Pane

Schema (/logs/metrics/2025-10-19.json):

{
  "runtimeCalls": 84,
  "aiCalls": 192,
  "avgLatencyMs": 1540,
  "failures": 3
}

11.8 Security
Rule	Enforcement
No cross-origin POSTs without API key	CORS whitelist
Limit 5MB request body	express.json()
No file uploads outside /workspace	path whitelist
Disable directory listing	middleware
Log all unauthorized requests	/logs/security.log
